1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,051
Here is a word: banana. In the middle, the little block A N appears twice in a row.

2
00:00:06,051 --> 00:00:11,664
A block followed straight away by an exact copy of itself is called a square.

3
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:20,097
Squares can be tiny, like two A's side by side, or long, like A B C, A B C.

4
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:26,111
So here is a puzzle. Can you write an endless string of letters with no square anywhere

5
00:00:26,111 --> 00:00:29,875
in it? The answer depends on how many letters you may use.

6
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:34,608
Try it with two letters, A and B. Start with A.

7
00:00:34,608 --> 00:00:39,782
The next letter can't be A, that would make a square, so write B.

8
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:42,171
Then A again. So far, so good.

9
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:47,513
Now add a fourth letter. If you add A, the word ends A, A: a square.

10
00:00:47,513 --> 00:00:51,586
If you add B, the whole word is A B, A B: also a square.

11
00:00:51,586 --> 00:00:54,423
Starting with B just swaps the letters.

12
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,053
So with two letters, three is as long as you can go.

13
00:00:59,053 --> 00:01:01,016
Squares are unavoidable.

14
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,449
With three letters, everything changes.

15
00:01:04,449 --> 00:01:09,848
Over a hundred years ago, the Norwegian mathematician Axel Thue proved that you can go

16
00:01:09,848 --> 00:01:15,248
on forever without a single square. Here is the start of one endless square-free word.

17
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:21,614
Some blocks nearly repeat. A B C A B starts its pattern again but stops early.

18
00:01:21,614 --> 00:01:27,748
It is five letters long and repeats every three, so its exponent is five thirds.

19
00:01:27,748 --> 00:01:30,201
A square has exponent exactly 2.

20
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:36,503
In 1972, Francoise Dejean showed that three letters can do even better: there are endless

21
00:01:36,503 --> 00:01:40,151
words where no block has exponent above seven quarters.

22
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:46,054
The word on screen is one. Its most repetitive blocks, like this one, reach exactly seven

23
00:01:46,054 --> 00:01:47,712
quarters, and no further.

24
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:53,500
Keep this word in mind. It is the raw material for everything that follows.

25
00:01:54,500 --> 00:02:01,151
Now a twist. Think of numbers, and draw each one as the height of a column.

26
00:02:01,151 --> 00:02:05,053
3, 1, 4, 1, 5 gives a little mountain range.

27
00:02:05,053 --> 00:02:07,004
So does 2, 0, 7, 0, 9.

28
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:11,154
The numbers are different, but the ups and downs look alike.

29
00:02:11,154 --> 00:02:16,160
To say precisely that they have the same shape, every column gets a code number.

30
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:21,749
Stand on a column and walk left, hopping over every column taller than you.

31
00:02:21,749 --> 00:02:27,799
Stop at the first one that is the same height or shorter, and write down how many steps

32
00:02:27,799 --> 00:02:30,789
back it was. If you never stop, write zero.

33
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:36,161
For 3, 1, 4, 1, 5 the code is 0, 0, 1, 2, 1.

34
00:02:36,161 --> 00:02:41,269
The second range gives exactly the same code.

35
00:02:41,269 --> 00:02:43,767
Same code, same shape.

36
00:02:44,167 --> 00:02:50,292
Computer scientists call this shape a Cartesian tree, and use it to search prices or

37
00:02:50,292 --> 00:02:55,615
sensor data for a pattern of rises and falls, whatever the actual values.

38
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:00,816
Back to letters, now 0 and 1, with 0 lower than 1.

39
00:03:00,816 --> 00:03:05,369
Zero zero, zero one and one one all get the code 0, 1.

40
00:03:05,369 --> 00:03:08,911
Nothing steps down, so they share a shape.

41
00:03:08,911 --> 00:03:12,368
One zero, which steps down, is different.

42
00:03:12,767 --> 00:03:15,444
Longer blocks have more interesting codes.

43
00:03:15,444 --> 00:03:20,735
In zero one one zero, the last zero hops over both ones and lands three steps back.

44
00:03:21,133 --> 00:03:27,143
A shape square is a block followed straight away by a block of the same shape.

45
00:03:27,143 --> 00:03:30,303
Zero one one one, then zero zero one one.

46
00:03:30,303 --> 00:03:34,232
The letters differ, but both codes read 0, 1, 1, 1.

47
00:03:34,232 --> 00:03:37,237
A shape square with halves of length 4.

48
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:43,303
Every ordinary square is also a shape square, so shape squares are harder to avoid.

49
00:03:43,303 --> 00:03:49,041
Tiny ones can't be avoided at all, since any two single letters have the same shape.

50
00:03:49,041 --> 00:03:50,953
So we only forbid long ones.

51
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:55,926
In 2026, Shibata, Mieno, Koeppl and Inenaga made a table.

52
00:03:55,926 --> 00:04:01,879
Each row is a number of letters, each column a minimum half length, and each entry is

53
00:04:01,879 --> 00:04:04,890
the longest word with no such shape square.

54
00:04:04,890 --> 00:04:06,501
Infinity means forever.

55
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:12,992
With two letters and halves of at least 3, the longest possible word has 29 letters.

56
00:04:12,992 --> 00:04:16,764
With three letters and halves of at least 2, only 9.

57
00:04:17,133 --> 00:04:22,497
But some entries were question marks. From experiments, the authors guessed infinity

58
00:04:22,497 --> 00:04:27,925
in two cells: two letters with halves of at least 4, and three letters with halves of

59
00:04:27,925 --> 00:04:31,437
at least 3. Those two cells would fill in all the rest.

60
00:04:31,833 --> 00:04:36,897
This finding proves both guesses. Every question mark becomes infinity.

61
00:04:38,467 --> 00:04:44,724
How do you build such a word? Start from Dejean's word over A, B and C, and replace each

62
00:04:44,724 --> 00:04:48,067
letter by a fixed block of five zeros and ones.

63
00:04:48,467 --> 00:04:53,636
Every A becomes this block. Every B becomes this one.

64
00:04:53,636 --> 00:04:56,075
Every C becomes this one.

65
00:04:56,467 --> 00:05:02,061
Apply the rule letter by letter, and the endless three letter word turns into an endless

66
00:05:02,061 --> 00:05:03,523
word of zeros and ones.

67
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:09,878
For three letters there is a similar rule, with blocks of six built from 0, 1 and 2.

68
00:05:09,878 --> 00:05:14,148
We follow the two letter case; the other works the same way.

69
00:05:14,533 --> 00:05:20,827
Short shape squares still appear. One one one, then zero one one: both have code 0, 1,

70
00:05:20,827 --> 00:05:27,048
1. That is allowed, since these halves have only three letters, and the table says it

71
00:05:27,048 --> 00:05:30,853
must happen. The claim is about halves of 4 or more.

72
00:05:31,833 --> 00:05:35,598
Why should that be true? The key is a sliding window.

73
00:05:35,598 --> 00:05:40,641
Look at a few letters at a time, and work out the code of what you see.

74
00:05:41,033 --> 00:05:47,251
First try five letters. Zero zero one one one, and zero zero zero zero one, have the

75
00:05:47,251 --> 00:05:50,138
same code but different middle letters.

76
00:05:50,138 --> 00:05:51,545
Five is not enough.

77
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:56,166
So try seven. A computer listed every seven letter window that can occur.

78
00:05:56,166 --> 00:06:00,806
There are 23 different codes, and each always comes with the same middle letter.

79
00:06:00,806 --> 00:06:04,749
Here, the shape of seven letters tells you the letter in the middle.

80
00:06:05,733 --> 00:06:11,748
Now suppose our word did contain a long shape square: two neighbouring blocks with the

81
00:06:11,748 --> 00:06:14,685
same code. Call the length of each half m.

82
00:06:15,066 --> 00:06:21,195
Put the second half under the first, and slide a seven letter window along both at once.

83
00:06:21,195 --> 00:06:27,255
Matching pieces of matching halves have matching codes, so each pair of windows has the

84
00:06:27,255 --> 00:06:28,578
same middle letter.

85
00:06:28,966 --> 00:06:34,746
Do this all the way along. Apart from three letters at each end, where the window doesn't

86
00:06:34,746 --> 00:06:37,863
fit, the two halves are equal letter for letter.

87
00:06:37,863 --> 00:06:42,214
A long shape square is really an ordinary square with blurry edges.

88
00:06:43,199 --> 00:06:48,806
Now the blocks matter. They were chosen so that a block never appears straddling two

89
00:06:48,806 --> 00:06:54,547
others. Slide zero zero one zero one across a pair of blocks: it only fits at the real

90
00:06:54,547 --> 00:06:58,151
boundary. Every block you see came from a real letter.

91
00:06:58,966 --> 00:07:02,520
The equal stretch is long, so it contains whole blocks.

92
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:08,077
Their copies in the second half must be real blocks too, so the two halves are shifted

93
00:07:08,077 --> 00:07:09,886
by a whole number of blocks.

94
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:12,898
Now read each block back as its letter.

95
00:07:12,898 --> 00:07:18,566
Matching blocks mean matching letters, so Dejean's word would contain a stretch that

96
00:07:18,566 --> 00:07:19,578
repeats itself.

97
00:07:19,966 --> 00:07:26,453
How much does it repeat? At most 14 letters are lost: 3 at each end, plus up to 4 more

98
00:07:26,453 --> 00:07:33,015
at each end to reach a whole block. That gives an exponent of at least 2, minus 14 over

99
00:07:33,015 --> 00:07:33,166
m.

100
00:07:33,566 --> 00:07:37,577
When m is bigger than 56, that is more than seven quarters.

101
00:07:37,577 --> 00:07:40,976
But Dejean's word never goes above seven quarters.

102
00:07:40,976 --> 00:07:45,734
Contradiction. So there is no shape square with halves longer than 56.

103
00:07:46,732 --> 00:07:49,782
That leaves halves from 4 to 56 letters.

104
00:07:49,782 --> 00:07:56,263
Any such square sits inside the blocks of just 24 consecutive letters of the starting

105
00:07:56,263 --> 00:07:56,644
word.

106
00:07:57,032 --> 00:08:04,325
So the computer took every 24 letter word over A, B and C with no block of exponent above

107
00:08:04,325 --> 00:08:07,602
seven quarters. There are 4,596 of them.

108
00:08:07,602 --> 00:08:14,485
In each one's zeros and ones it searched for shape squares with halves from 4 to 56.

109
00:08:14,485 --> 00:08:15,632
It found none.

110
00:08:15,999 --> 00:08:23,850
For the three letter rule, the same kind of check covers 3,654 words, with halves from

111
00:08:23,850 --> 00:08:25,767
3 to 64. Again, none.

112
00:08:26,165 --> 00:08:31,392
Two separately written programs agree on every count, and both reject rules that were

113
00:08:31,392 --> 00:08:32,621
deliberately broken.

114
00:08:33,599 --> 00:08:39,180
So what kind of proof is this? The endless part is the written argument you just watched.

115
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:42,567
The computer only checks a finite list of short cases.

116
00:08:42,567 --> 00:08:46,078
Together they make a proof, but a computer assisted one.

117
00:08:46,078 --> 00:08:51,346
It has not been checked in a proof assistant like Lean, and it has not yet been peer

118
00:08:51,346 --> 00:08:51,911
reviewed.

119
00:08:52,299 --> 00:08:58,585
It is also modest. It answers two questions left open in a recent paper and completes

120
00:08:58,585 --> 00:09:05,019
its table. And the thresholds can't be lowered: the table's 29 and 9 already show that.

121
00:09:05,999 --> 00:09:10,917
To recap. One: two letters can go on forever avoiding shape squares with halves of 4

122
00:09:10,917 --> 00:09:14,372
or more, and three letters, those with halves of 3 or more.

123
00:09:14,372 --> 00:09:18,471
Two: the words come from Dejean's word by swapping letters for blocks.

124
00:09:18,471 --> 00:09:23,389
Three: a seven letter window turns long shape squares into near-exact squares, which

125
00:09:23,389 --> 00:09:26,903
Dejean's word forbids, and a computer clears the short ones.

126
00:09:27,299 --> 00:09:32,329
What we still don't know: how close to a shape square an endless word must come, and

127
00:09:32,329 --> 00:09:36,042
how rare shape squares can be made when they can't be avoided.

128
00:09:36,042 --> 00:09:39,515
Those are the next questions the original authors suggest.
